{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Tests are proof projections from admitted work authority. A test expectation and the separate executed check have distinct authority, derivation and execution-provenance relations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1072,
          "quote": "Tests are proof projections from admitted work authority.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FTicket%20Rules%2FTest%20Case%20Authority",
          "start_line": 1072
        }
      ],
      "key": "test_expectation",
      "label": "Test expectation and proof authority"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A durable unit of work carrying its state, admitted change intent, classification, re-entry, truth changes, evaluation criteria and closure conditions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1106,
          "quote": "1. the ticket's `change_intent`, `change_class`, `re_entry_point`,\n   `target_truth`, `superseded_truth`, `closure_law`,\n   `evaluation_criteria`, and `non_closure_conditions`",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FTicket%20Rules%2FTest%20Case%20Authority",
          "start_line": 1104
        }
      ],
      "key": "ticket",
      "label": "Ticket"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "ticket_category: implementation_migration"
      ],
      "definition": "Ticket category supplying the concrete declaration, break/closure map and review checklist as evidence to Spec's migration law and shared preservation contract. It is a local enforcement surface, not a competing migration policy.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1137,
          "quote": "shared preservation contract. This ticket family chooses the concrete\ndeclaration, break/closure map and review checklist; it supplies evidence to\nthat law, not another migration policy or permission to reinterpret it.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FTicket%20Rules%2FImplementation%20Migration%20Category",
          "start_line": 1135
        }
      ],
      "key": "implementation_migration_ticket",
      "label": "Implementation migration ticket"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Migration strategy field with the declared alternatives inside_out_hard_break and fundamental_re_adoption; their respective required evidence and declarations differ.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1196,
          "quote": "- `inside_out_hard_break`\n- `fundamental_re_adoption`",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FTicket%20Rules%2FImplementation%20Migration%20Category",
          "start_line": 1195
        }
      ],
      "key": "migration_strategy",
      "label": "Migration strategy"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "library_usage records whether the active migration boundary rebuilds a pattern locally, consumes a reusable library/commonization surface, or extends that surface before local implementation lands.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1286,
          "quote": "Implementation migration tickets must declare whether the active boundary is:\n\n- rebuilding a pattern locally\n- consuming an existing reusable library/commonization surface\n- extending that surface before the local implementation lands\n\nThis is what `library_usage` records.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FTicket%20Rules%2FRecurring%20Realization%20And%20Library%20Declaration",
          "start_line": 1280
        }
      ],
      "key": "library_usage",
      "label": "Library usage declaration"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Core-interface, carrier, or authoritative-contract migration"
      ],
      "definition": "A named walk of every live interface that can bypass, reinterpret, replay or project the migrating truth path, with an interface-specific closure condition for each item.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1370,
          "quote": "This checklist is a named walk of every live interface that can still bypass,\nreinterpret, replay, or project the migrating truth path.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FTicket%20Rules%2FImpacted%20Interface%20Review%20Checklist",
          "start_line": 1369
        }
      ],
      "key": "impacted_interface_checklist",
      "label": "Impacted Interface Review Checklist"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Work-carrier states have the minimum values backlog, active and completed. They are recorded work state, not a second result algebra. A state-transition validity claim has its own exact predicate and core result.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1488,
          "quote": "These values select work-carrier state, not a second result algebra. A claim\nthat a state transition is valid carries its own core result and exact\npredicate.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FTicket%20Rules%2FAllowed%20Status",
          "start_line": 1486
        }
      ],
      "key": "ticket_status",
      "label": "Ticket status"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Product Definition Overlay bindings ticketing.tickets.lanes.backlog, .active and .completed locate the respective lanes. Defaults are .ai-workspace/tickets/backlog/, .ai-workspace/tickets/active/ and .ai-workspace/tickets/completed/. Backlog means accepted durable visible work outside current execution focus; active means the current bounded execution set; completed means closed work retained as history. Keeping active work small supports fast, readable rg without putting deferred work into comments or goals.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1517,
          "quote": "- `backlog`: accepted durable work that should remain visible but is not part\n  of the current execution focus\n- `active`: the current bounded execution set\n- `completed`: closed work retained as history\n\nThis keeps the live execution set small enough that `rg` remains fast and\nreadable without forcing deferred work into comments or goals.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FTicket%20Rules%2FTicket%20Lanes",
          "start_line": 1511
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1507,
          "quote": "Backlog tickets live at the Product Definition Overlay's\n`ticketing.tickets.lanes.backlog` binding. The default is:\n\n- `.ai-workspace/tickets/backlog/`\n\nActive tickets live at `ticketing.tickets.lanes.active`. The default is:\n\n- `.ai-workspace/tickets/active/`\n\nCompleted tickets move to `ticketing.tickets.lanes.completed`. The default is:\n\n- `.ai-workspace/tickets/completed/`",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FTicket%20Rules%2FTicket%20Lanes",
          "start_line": 1496
        }
      ],
      "key": "ticket_lanes",
      "label": "Ticket lanes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "UP-009's work-state authority account assigns the bounded work wave to Goals, ticket state to the ticket, subordinate milestone state to milestone authority, and realization decisions to accepted design. Comments, dashboards, registers, indexes and generated summaries are projections.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1529,
          "quote": "Work-carrier state has one authority chain: Goals own the bounded work wave, the ticket\nowns ticket state, milestone authority owns subordinate milestone state, and\naccepted design owns realization decisions. Comments, dashboards, registers,\nindexes, and generated summaries are projections.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FTicket%20Rules%2FOne-Truth%20State%20Projection%20And%20Drift%20Refusal%20%28%60STDO-UP-009%60%29",
          "start_line": 1526
        }
      ],
      "key": "work_state_authority",
      "label": "One-truth work-state authority"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Illustrative recommended sprint shape, not an instantiated sprint"
      ],
      "definition": "SPRINT-2026-04-30-operational-projection is an active operational-projection-ux example opened and updated at 2026-04-30T09:00:00Z. It cites GOALS.md#operational-projection-ux, PRODUCT.md#operational-projection and a governing-design-surface/OPERATIONAL_PROJECTION.md placeholder. Scope is layout, readability, interaction affordance and visual evidence quality; expected change class is realization_refactor. UXI-001 improves relation-line readability, cites screenshots/2026-04-30/UXI-001.png and records pending accessibility contrast debt.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1550,
          "quote": "- id: SPRINT-2026-04-30-operational-projection\n- status: active\n- goal: operational-projection-ux\n- opened_at: 2026-04-30T09:00:00Z\n- updated_at: 2026-04-30T09:00:00Z",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FRecommended%20Ticket%20Shape",
          "start_line": 1546
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1579,
          "quote": "- id: UXI-001\n  intent: improve relation-line readability\n  evidence_ref: screenshots/2026-04-30/UXI-001.png\n  debt: accessibility contrast check pending",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FRecommended%20Ticket%20Shape",
          "start_line": 1576
        }
      ],
      "key": "operational_projection_sprint_example",
      "label": "Recommended operational projection sprint example"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Illustrative recommended ticket shape"
      ],
      "definition": "T-001 Add Product Layer To SPEC_METHOD is an ordinary active high-priority feature for methodology-bootstrap. Its intent is to make the product-definition layer explicit in the constitutional chain; change_class is product_reprice and re_entry_point is product. Triaged, created and updated dates are 2026-04-07. Context says SPEC_METHOD.md omitted that layer. Acceptance lists PRODUCT.md in the constitutional chain, intent -> product -> requirements in bootstrap, and examples/reconstruction reflecting the layer. A comment link uses .ai-workspace/comments/<agent-id>/.... These are example claims, not verified facts about external files.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1601,
          "quote": "- id: T-001\n- type: feature\n- ticket_category: ordinary\n- status: active\n- goal: methodology-bootstrap\n- change_intent: make the product-definition layer explicit in the constitutional chain\n- change_class: product_reprice\n- re_entry_point: product",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FRecommended%20Ticket%20Shape",
          "start_line": 1594
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1616,
          "quote": "- `PRODUCT.md` is part of the constitutional chain\n- bootstrap sequence includes `intent -> product -> requirements`\n- examples and reconstruction logic reflect the new layer",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FRecommended%20Ticket%20Shape",
          "start_line": 1614
        }
      ],
      "key": "product_layer_ticket_example",
      "label": "Recommended Product-layer ticket example"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Illustrative implementation migration ticket"
      ],
      "definition": "B-010 Replace Mixed Gap Projection With One Authoritative Carrier is an active bug, implementation_migration, inside_out_hard_break, library_usage none with a boundary-specific/nonrecurring rationale. Goal is operator-gap-truth, intent replaces mixed operator gap truth with one authoritative declared carrier, change_class design_reframe, re_entry_point design_surface, and triaged/created/updated dates 2026-04-20. Scope is gap projection on the declared operator-query acceptance path, with no excluded/disjoint scopes. Old truth is fallback gap projection with synthetic convergence fields; new truth is explicit declared-obligation projection plus separately classified fallback gaps. Old producer is <gap-module>::build_fallback_gap; new producers are <obligation-module>::collect_declared_obligations and <gap-module>::build_canonical_gap. Both consumer sets are <query-module>::project_gaps, <summary-module>::project_span and <review-surface>::render. Derived surfaces are <query-surface>::gaps, <summary-surface>::span and <proof-surface>::first_slice_negative.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1641,
          "quote": "- id: B-010\n- type: bug\n- ticket_category: implementation_migration\n- migration_strategy: inside_out_hard_break\n- library_usage: none\n- library_rationale: this migration is boundary-specific and does not establish a recurring realization pattern\n- status: active\n- goal: operator-gap-truth\n- change_intent: replace the mixed operator gap truth with one authoritative declared carrier\n- change_class: design_reframe\n- re_entry_point: design_surface\n- triaged_at: 2026-04-20\n- created_at: 2026-04-20\n- updated_at: 2026-04-20",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FRecommended%20Ticket%20Shape",
          "start_line": 1628
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1656,
          "quote": "- affected_scope: gap projection on the declared operator-query acceptance path\n- excluded_or_disjoint_scopes: none\n- old_truth_path: fallback gap projection with synthetic convergence fields\n- new_truth_path: explicit declared-obligation projection plus separately classified fallback gaps\n- producers_old: `<gap-module>::build_fallback_gap`\n- producers_new: `<obligation-module>::collect_declared_obligations`, `<gap-module>::build_canonical_gap`\n- consumers_old: `<query-module>::project_gaps`, `<summary-module>::project_span`, `<review-surface>::render`\n- consumers_new: `<query-module>::project_gaps`, `<summary-module>::project_span`, `<review-surface>::render`\n- derived_surfaces:\n  - `<query-surface>::gaps`\n  - `<summary-surface>::span`\n  - `<proof-surface>::first_slice_negative`",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FRecommended%20Ticket%20Shape",
          "start_line": 1645
        }
      ],
      "key": "gap_migration_ticket_example",
      "label": "Recommended gap migration ticket example"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Default local-file carrier selected"
      ],
      "definition": "The default carrier assumes humans and agents directly read active tickets, backlog stays searchable durable work outside immediate focus, rg suffices to find active work, and no database or board is required. The three bash examples search active/backlog headings and status/type/goal fields, or ticket IDs T-001 and B-002 across ticket files; they are illustrative commands.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1687,
          "quote": "The default local-file carrier assumes:\n\n- humans and agents can read the active ticket folder directly\n- backlog remains searchable durable work, but does not compete with the active\n  lane for immediate attention\n- `rg` is sufficient for finding active work\n- no database or board is required",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FOperating%20Mode",
          "start_line": 1681
        }
      ],
      "key": "local_file_ticket_carrier",
      "label": "Default local-file ticket carrier"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "GOALS.md identifies the broad current work wave; SPEC_METHOD.md governs change class and re-entry; sprints identify bounded execution batches pricing proof, evidence and paydown; tickets identify durable work units and state; comments record proposals, argument, decisions, review and handoffs; POSTING_GUIDE.md structures and names comment publications. Separation prevents overloaded goals, deferred work diluting active work, comments becoming informal status and boards becoming accidental authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1709,
          "quote": "- `GOALS.md` answers: what broad work wave matters now\n- `SPEC_METHOD.md` answers: what constitutional change class and re-entry point\n  govern the work\n- sprints answer: what bounded execution batch is currently pricing proof,\n  evidence, and paydown\n- tickets answer: what durable units of work exist and what state they are in\n- comments answer: what was proposed, argued, decided, reviewed, or handed off\n- `POSTING_GUIDE.md` answers: how comment-layer publications are structured and\n  named",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TICKET_METHOD.md#Ticket%20Method%2FRelationship%20To%20Other%20Surfaces",
          "start_line": 1701
        }
      ],
      "key": "work_surface_roles",
      "label": "Distinct work-surface roles"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Selected foundational premises and instance choices"
      ],
      "definition": "The Transformation Library owns shared application, projection and composition contracts for purposeful specification-governed construction. Its context is urn:stdo:bounded-context:transformation-library, owned by STDO Specification Methodology Product authority. Its basis is the exact member digest and cited source-owned contracts under the activation's governing cut. Scope includes shared deciding contracts, conditional specialization arguments and explicitly imported authority vocabulary. Bind, Acquire, Transform, Evaluate, Conjoin and Dispose are composable or co-locatable responsibilities, not mandatory stages, actors or runtime APIs.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 7,
          "quote": "This member owns the shared transformation contracts for the\npurposeful, specification-governed construction domain declared by\n[Foundations](FOUNDATIONS.md#foundational-commitments). Bind, Acquire, Transform,\nEvaluate, Conjoin and Dispose are responsibilities that can be composed or\nco-located, not compulsory stages, actors or runtime APIs.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TRANSFORMATION_LIBRARY.md#Transformation%20Library",
          "start_line": 3
        },
        {
          "end_line": 21,
          "quote": "Its shared contract vocabulary resolves in bounded context\n`urn:stdo:bounded-context:transformation-library`, owned by the STDO Specification\nMethodology Product authority. The basis is this exact member digest and its\ncited source-owned contracts under the activation's governing cut; its scope\nis the shared deciding contracts, conditional specialization arguments and\nthe explicitly imported authority vocabulary.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TRANSFORMATION_LIBRARY.md#Transformation%20Library",
          "start_line": 16
        }
      ],
      "key": "transformation_library",
      "label": "Transformation Library"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Owner-qualified references from urn:stdo:bounded-context:reference-frame-evaluation distinguish frame-set authority (human declaring/accepting a project frame set), semantic authority (meaning owner for a material relation), evaluation authority (role permitted to evaluate), operation authority (owner-issued grant to construct, transform, admit, publish or mutate a material subject where evaluation performs that operation), decision authority (role permitted to accept, reject or otherwise dispose a result), and actor identity (human, agent, tool or process performing work).",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 52,
          "quote": "| frame-set authority | urn:stdo:bounded-context:reference-frame-evaluation | [Frame authority](REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD.md#authority): the human authority that declares or accepts the project frame set |\n| semantic authority | urn:stdo:bounded-context:reference-frame-evaluation | [Frame authority](REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD.md#authority): the owner of meaning for a material relation |\n| evaluation authority | urn:stdo:bounded-context:reference-frame-evaluation | [Frame authority](REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD.md#authority): the role permitted to perform an evaluation |\n| operation authority | urn:stdo:bounded-context:reference-frame-evaluation | [Frame authority](REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD.md#authority): the owner-issued grant to construct, transform, admit, publish, or mutate a material subject where an evaluation performs such an operation |\n| decision authority | urn:stdo:bounded-context:reference-frame-evaluation | [Frame authority](REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD.md#authority): the role permitted to accept, reject, or otherwise dispose its result |\n| actor identity | urn:stdo:bounded-context:reference-frame-evaluation | [Frame authority](REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD.md#authority): the human, agent, tool, or process performing work. |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TRANSFORMATION_LIBRARY.md#Transformation%20Library%2FReference-only%20semantic%20imports",
          "start_line": 47
        }
      ],
      "key": "imported_authority_vocabulary",
      "label": "Reference-only authority vocabulary"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "One selected operation"
      ],
      "definition": "Operation parameters X and Y are declared input/output domains, K interpretation, P required outcome predicate, I material identity/state relations and G actual effect or decision grant. They are semantic parameters, not serialized fields or a universal schema. Instance-owned original domain outcomes have a total refinement into the referenced core result algebra; unknown judgment, non-performance and uncertain effects retain separate coordinates.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 75,
          "quote": "For one selected operation, let X and Y be its declared input and output\ndomains, K its interpretation, P its required outcome predicate, I its material\nidentity/state relations and G its actual effect or decision grant. These are\nparameters, not serialized fields or a universal schema. The instance owns its original domain outcomes and their total refinement into\n[the core result algebra](FOUNDATIONS.md#result-algebra). Unknown judgment,\nnon-performance and uncertain effects retain their separate coordinates.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TRANSFORMATION_LIBRARY.md#Transformation%20Library%2FFoundational%20contract",
          "start_line": 70
        }
      ],
      "key": "operation_contract_coordinates",
      "label": "Selected operation coordinates"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Contract application determines whether a proposed operation or reliance is admissible under a bound contract; this differs from achieving its intended outcome. A contract binds domains, predicate, governing constraints, material relations, evidence rule, actual grants, owners and current basis. Existing design, frame, function or work carriers can provide these coordinates without an additional carrier or validation stage.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 127,
          "quote": "A contract bounds an operation by its input and output domains, intended\npredicate, governing constraints, material relations, evidence rule and actual\ngrants. Its binding names their owners and current basis. These are semantic\ncoordinates, not a required record format. An existing design, frame, function\nor work carrier can supply them; this law requires no additional carrier or\nvalidation stage.\n\nThe application predicate is: **this proposed operation or reliance is\nadmissible under this bound contract**. It differs from the predicate that the\noperation achieves its intended outcome. Application uses the following\nconditions once at the responsible consumer:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TRANSFORMATION_LIBRARY.md#Transformation%20Library%2FContract%20application",
          "start_line": 117
        }
      ],
      "key": "contract_application",
      "label": "Contract application"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Current subject and basis"
      ],
      "definition": "A task's constraint surface describes work allowed by the bound contract's constraints and grants, unresolved obligations, and the distinction between permitted choices and choices reserved to an owner.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 194,
          "quote": "For a current subject and basis, the bound contract describes the work allowed\nby its constraints and grants. It also exposes obligations still unresolved.\nThis is the task's constraint surface. It distinguishes the permitted choices\ninside the contract from the choices reserved to an owner. A missing condition\nis not freedom; a condition proved irrelevant to this task need not be loaded\nagain while that proof remains applicable.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/TRANSFORMATION_LIBRARY.md#Transformation%20Library%2FContract%20application%2FConstraint%20surface",
          "start_line": 189
        }
      ],
      "key": "constraint_surface",
      "label": "Constraint surface"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Projection or interpretation applies Contract application to a declared source-to-consumer relation binding authoritative source and selector, target interpretation, consumer task, required distinctions, admitted loss/approximation and invalidation conditions. Source and target retain distinct identities; publication and adoption conditions remain domain-owned.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 217,
          "quote": "A projection or interpretation applies Contract application to a declared\nsource-to-consumer relation. Its binding states the authoritative source and\nselector, target interpretation, consumer task, required distinctions, admitted\nloss or approximation, and invalidation conditions. Source and target retain\ntheir own identities. The domain owns additional publication and adoption\nconditions.",
